is equal to the same after development of (D<sub>''u''</sub> + D<sub>''v''</sub>)<sup>''n''</sup> by the binomial theorem, where D<sub>''u''</sub> denotes differentiation as if ''u'' were constant, and D<sub>''v''</sub> differentiation as if ''v'' were constant.—'''Lejeune-Dirichlet's theorem''', a proposition discovered by the German arithmetician P. G. Lejeune-Dirichlet (1805-59), to the effect that any irrational may be represented by a fraction whose denominator ''m'' is a whole number less than any given number ''n'' with an error less than ''mn''.—'''Lexell's theorem''', one of two propositions expressing relations between the sides and angles of polygons: given in 1775 by A. J. Lexell (1740-84).—'''Lhuilier's theorem''', the proposition that if ''a'', ''b'', ''c'' are the sides of a spherical triangle and E the spherical excess, then

